{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310849,"nonce":"THWLte6j5/G0V7igiKVnO1Ta1/4=","title":"MEASURED expert locality on Qwen3.5-35B-A3B, single AND multi-stream: CONCURRENT SPOKES DO NOT MULTIPLY THE WORKING SET (6 spokes = 31% of ...","message":"**MEASURED 2026-08-19** by hooking `Qwen3NextSparseMoeBlock.__call__` in mlx_lm\n(Qwen3.5 routes through `qwen3_next.Qwen3NextSparseMoeBlock`, imported by\n`qwen3_5.py`; capture the `mx.argpartition(gates, kth=-k)` indices). 5280 routing\nevents, 40 MoE layers, 96 generated tokens, 256 experts, top-8.\n\n**LOCALITY IS HIGH — THIS IS THE RESULT THE PRODUCT DEPENDS ON.**\n- **69.8% of a token's 8 experts were also used by the previous token.**\n- Working set over 96 tokens: 82 distinct experts per layer of 256.\n- **Steady state (last 48 tokens, cache warmed on the first 48): only 25\n  distinct experts per layer.**\n\n**LRU EXPERT CACHE, STEADY STATE:**\n\n| cache/layer | expert RAM | hit | misses/token | disk/token | ~ms @2GB/s |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 16 | 0.58 GB | 94.2% | 18.7 | 17.0 MB | 8.5 |\n| **32** | **1.16 GB** | **97.4%** | **8.4** | **7.6 MB** | **3.8** |\n| 64 | 2.32 GB | 98.2% | 5.9 | 5.4 MB | 2.7 |\n\nAgainst 320 expert reads per token (40 layers x top-8) and a ~41 ms/token budget\nat the measured 24 tok/s, a few ms of streaming is affordable.\n\n**BUDGET: the 10.2 GiB resident model becomes ~3 GB** — trunk (~2.4B params,\nkept at higher precision in a UD quant, ~1.2 GB) + 1.16 GB expert cache + ~0.5 GB\nKV/activations. **That fits an 8 GB machine**, which the fully-resident 10.2 GiB\ndoes not.\n\n**MEASURE IT WARMED OR YOU WILL UNDERSTATE IT BADLY.** A cold LRU over 96 tokens\nplateaus at **89.4%** no matter the cache size — that ceiling is exactly\n`1 - 82/768`, i.e. COMPULSORY first-touch misses of a short run, not a property\nof the access pattern. Oracle \"keep the M hottest\" gave 97.7% at M=64; cold LRU\ngave 89.2%; warmed LRU gives 98.2%. Three numbers, three meanings — quote the\nwarmed one for steady-state serving.\n\n**CAVEATS, stated:** one prompt, one domain, 96 tokens; steady state measured\nover 48 tokens. Locality across topic switches is UNMEASURED and is the obvious\nnext probe. Disk time is arithmetic at an assumed 2 GB/s, not measured — and\nthese are ~0.9 MB RANDOM reads. Per-expert streaming also assumes an expert's\nweights are contiguous and individually addressable in the file.\n\n**WHAT IT MEANS FOR NueGraph:** residency is now a measured requirement with a\nsize attached. Stage 3 (lifetimes/residency) needs an expert cache with an\neviction policy, and Stage 4 needs to place the resident set on GPU while\nstreaming the tail. mmap alone gives the OS's policy, not 97% at 1.16 GB.\n\n---\n\n\nThe open question was whether N concurrent spokes on DIFFERENT topics each need\ntheir own working set. **They do not.** Captured 6 unrelated prompts (db,\ncooking, biology, code, poetry, finance) x 192 decode tokens in ONE model load\n(`scratchpad/capture_multi.py`, dumps after EVERY prompt so a failure cannot\ncost a 10 GB load).\n\n**LRU hit rate, k spokes interleaved, warmed on the first half:**\n\n| expert cache | +0.73 floor | k=1 | k=2 | k=4 | k=6 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1.0 GB | 1.73 GB | 97.4% | 97.3% | **97.1%** | 84.4% |\n| 2.0 GB | 2.73 GB | 98.6% | 99.1% | **99.4%** | 96.1% |\n| 3.0 GB | 3.73 GB | 98.9% | 99.3% | 99.6% | **99.3%** |\n\n**4 spokes cost essentially NOTHING over 1 spoke** (97.1% vs 97.4% at 1 GB).\nUnion working set: k=6 is 6.52 GB vs 20.85 GB if isolated = **31.3%**. Pairwise\nJaccard overlap between unrelated topics is 29-60% (code is the outlier, lowest\noverlap and smallest working set).\n\n**DISPATCH GRANULARITY IS IRRELEVANT.** Interleaving spokes per LAYER (what a\nserialized-dispatch pool produces) scores within 0.2 pt of interleaving per\nTOKEN at every size. Do NOT design around fine-grained interleaving hurting\ncache locality — measured, it does not.\n\n**ACCOUNTING:** expert instance = 0.94 MB (3 x 2048 x 512 params @ 2 bit +\nfp16 group scale/bias, group 64). 256 experts x 40 layers = 9.38 GiB. Always-\nresident floor (attn, norms, embed, lm_head, and the 40 SHARED experts that run\nfor every token) = **0.73 GiB**. Total = 10.10 GiB safetensors.\n\n**COST OF A MISS:** 0.36 ms for one expert-sized random read (2.57 GB/s, page\ncache possibly warm so this is an UPPER BOUND ON SPEED). 320 expert accesses per\ntoken per spoke. k=4 @ 2 GB = 1.9 misses/token = +0.7 ms (~1% of a 62 ms token).\nk=6 @ 1 GB = 50 misses/token = +18 ms (+29%) — that is the only cliff in range.\n\n**TRAP THAT COST A RE-CAPTURE: WARM-UP LENGTH.** 48-token streams gave k=1 =\n92.2% at 1 GB; 192-token streams gave **97.4%**, reproducing the single-stream\nnumber exactly. Short streams are PESSIMISTIC. Use \u003e=150 decode tokens.\n\n**TRAP: PREFILL IS LAYER-MAJOR.** In a capture, prefill emits all prompt\npositions for layer 0, then layer 1... That ordering has far better locality\nthan real decode and inflates the hit rate if mixed in. Separate on the prefill\nflag and measure DECODE ONLY. (The old single-stream capture had no flag; its\nprefill is the FIRST `len(recs) - ntok*L` events.)\n\n**DATA IS NOT DEGENERATE:** all 256 experts used in every stream, selection\nentropy 7.71-7.86 bits against a uniform max of 8.0, no expert above 1.3% of\npicks.\n\n**ENVIRONMENT:** mlx_lm is NOT installed system-wide and did not survive the\nearlier session — venv at `scratchpad/mlxenv` (mlx-lm 0.31.3). Verify the hook\ntarget before loading 10 GB: `Qwen3NextSparseMoeBlock` with `self.gate`,\n`self.top_k`, `mx.argpartition(gates, kth=-k)` — confirmed present in 0.31.3.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_moe_expert_locality_measured` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}